To reproduce the results of `The demise of the world's largest piedmont glacier' by Brinkerhoff et al., take the following steps.

1. Go to https://zenodo.org/records/15028564 or to https://github.com/CompatibleElementGlacierModel/ManuscriptCode, and download the version of the SpecEIS glacier dynamics model found there (and its dependencies).

2. Place this directory (malaspina) in SpecEIS's examples directory.

3. Run the scripts in 'preprocessing' in the following order.  These generate a) the model's mesh; b) maps from whitened parameters to physical model parameters (i.e. bed, log_beta, smb); and 3) maps from model predictions to observations (velocity and surface elevation at points).  

mesh_boundary.py
build_bed.py
build_surface.py
build_adot.py
build_beta.py
build_velocity.py

4. Perform steady state inference as an initial guess.  This finds a bed, average beta, and surface mass balance such that the model is both in steady state and agrees with available observations c. 2012.  To do this, navigate to the malaspina/inference directory and run inference_steady.py.  This will take approximately 1 hour to run.  Model results can be visualized easily in paraview from the meshes/v1/init/adjoint directory.

5. Perform time-dependent inference using the result from previous as an initial guess.  This finds a bed, time-dependent beta, and time-dependent surface mass balance such that the model trajectory over the observational period is consistent with observations at relevant times.  To do this, run inference_time.py.  This will take approximately 8 hours to run.  Model results can be visualized in paraview from the meshes/v1/time/adjoint directory.

6. Perform uncertainty quantification.  This runs simulations on perturbed parameter values and takes their gradients to compute approximations to the Hessian-vector product of the cost function.  These will be used for uncertainty quantification.  To do this, run inference/sample_hvp.py.  This will take approximately 24 hours to run.  The resulting data is stored in meshes/v1/uncertainty_quantification/hvps

7. Perform ensemble simulations.  Construct 25 member, 300 year ensembles based on parameters drawn from the Laplace-approximated posterior distribution for each of the 4 experiments (calving on/off, frozen/projected smb).  To do this, run inference/run_all.py.  These runs are checkpointed at various times, and can be visualized via paraview in meshes/v1/ensemble.  Individual ensemble experiments can be run via inference/forward.py.

8. Plot all of the figures from the manuscript.  After previous runs are complete, navigate to postprocessing and run postprocessing/run_all.py.
